.TH ATOF 3
.SH NAME
atof, atoi, atol, strtol, strtoul \- convert ASCII to numbers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.B double atof(const char *nptr);
.PP
.B int atoi(const char *nptr);
.PP
.B long atol(const char *nptr);
.PP
.B long strtol(const char *nptr, char **endptr, int base);
.PP
.B unsigned long strtoul(const char *nptr, char **endptr, int base);
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions convert a string pointed to by
.I nptr
to floating, integer, and long integer
representation respectively.
The first unrecognized character ends the string.
.PP
.I Atof
recognizes an optional string of tabs and spaces,
then an optional sign, then
a string of digits optionally containing a decimal
point, then an optional `e' or `E' followed
by an optionally signed integer.
.PP
.I Atoi
and
.I atol
recognize an optional string of tabs and spaces,
then an optional sign, then a string of
digits.
.PP
.B Note:
.I strtol
and
.I strtoul
are C11 and had no v7 ancestor.  Each converts an initial subject sequence in
.I base
(2 to 36, or 0 to take the base from a leading "0x" or "0"), stores a pointer to
the first unconverted character through
.I endptr
if that is not NULL, and reports overflow by returning the appropriate limit
with
.I errno
set to ERANGE.  They are what a caller should use where
.I atoi
has "no provisions for overflow".
.SH SEE ALSO
scanf(3), ecvt(3), string(3)
.SH BUGS
There are no provisions for overflow
.I in the atoi family.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS atol is a call to atoi
.B long
is
.B int
here \- one word \- so the two conversions cannot differ by so much as an
overflow.  One word is also all the system-call gate carries, which is why
.IR lseek (2)
and
.IR stime (2)
each shed an argument v7's gate had.
.SS The limits are asymmetric and narrow
.B long
is 41 bits signed, so LONG_MAX is 2**40\-1 and LONG_MIN is \-2**40, while an
.B "unsigned long"
is the full 48 bits.  A page that quotes the PDP-11's 32767 or 2147483647 is
quoting a different machine.
.SS No signed value is ever reinterpreted as unsigned
An integer here is a 41-bit two's-complement field with bits 48..42
.IR zero ,
so the raw word of a negative value is
.I not
its 48-bit two's complement.  BSD's scanner builds its negative cutoff as
.B "\-(unsigned long)LONG_MIN"
and assigns LONG_MIN into an unsigned accumulator; both depend on how the front
end widens a sign, and here it
.I reinterprets
rather than sign-extends \-
.B (unsigned long)(\-1L) is 2**41\-1, not ULONG_MAX.
.PP
Every cast in
.I strtol
is value-preserving instead: the negative cutoff is
.BR "(unsigned long)LONG_MAX + 1" ,
and LONG_MIN is returned as the constant and never as
.BR "\-(long)acc" ,
the accumulator holding 2**40 there, which no
.B long
can.
.SS Two C11 fixes to the scanner it came from
"0x" with no hex digit after it converts the 0 and stops at the `x'.  BSD
consumes the prefix, finds nothing, and reports the whole input unconsumed.
.PP
A base outside 0 and 2..36 gives EINVAL.  C11 leaves it undefined and BSD scans
on regardless.
.SS atof's constants are this machine's
.I Big
is 2**40, not v7's 2**56, and LOGHUGE is 19 rather than 39 \- both mantissa and
exponent widths, and both wrong if carried over.  The local exponent variable is
spelled
.I dexp
because the front end has one namespace for objects and functions and <math.h>
has declared
.IR exp ().
.PP
.I Atof
also avoids the comma operator, which the front end evaluated to its
.I left
operand for a time \- so
.B "while (isdigit(c = *p++))"
read "3.5" as the three digits 3, \-2 and 5.  That is fixed upstream and the
spelling is now a preference rather than a workaround.
.SS strtoul is a second copy of the scanner, deliberately
One member per function is what lets the archive index pull only the one a
program calls, and a shared core would come along with either.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/atof.c, atoi.c, atol.c, strtol.c, strtoul.c
